## Mouse Interactions

Mouse handling is done manually:

1. Listen for `mousemove` events:
```542:545:demo.html
		document.addEventListener("mousemove", (event) => {
			mouseX = event.clientX;
			mouseY = event.clientY;
		});
```

2. Convert CSS coordinates to shader coordinates in `onBeforeFrame` using `ctx.toShaderX()` and `ctx.toShaderY()`:
```78:90:data/shader-web-background-main/demo/mouse.html
      onBeforeFrame: (ctx) => {
        time = performance.now() / 1000;

        // Note: shader (0, 0) pixel coordinates are actually (.5, .5)
        // and they are located at the bottom-left corner of the canvas.
        // Also the actual pixel-perfect resolution might differ from so
        // called CSS pixels depending on the device or zoom level. See:
        //
        // https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/devicePixelRatio
        //
        // therefore we need to translate to shader coordinates through the context
        shaderMouseX = ctx.toShaderX(mouseX);
        shaderMouseY = ctx.toShaderY(mouseY);
      },
```

3. Pass as uniforms to the shader:
```94:98:data/shader-web-background-main/demo/mouse.html
          uniforms: {
            iResolution:   (gl, loc, ctx) => gl.uniform2f(loc, ctx.width, ctx.height),
            iTime:         (gl, loc) => gl.uniform1f(loc, time),
            iMinDimension: (gl, loc) => gl.uniform1f(loc, minDimension),
            iMouse:        (gl, loc) => gl.uniform2f(loc, shaderMouseX, shaderMouseY)
          }
```

## Device Orientation / Gyroscope

Device orientation is handled similarly:

1. Listen for `deviceorientation` events:
```475:492:demo.html
		function handleDeviceOrientationChange(event) {
			if (event.beta) {
				if (tiltFBDelta) {
					tiltFB = event.beta - tiltFBDelta;
				} else {  // first reading or calibration
					tiltFBDelta = event.beta;
					tiltFB = 0;
				}
			}
			if (event.gamma) {
				tiltLR = event.gamma;
			}
		}

		const deviceOrientationEvent = "deviceorientation";

		function trackDeviceOrientation() {
			window.addEventListener(deviceOrientationEvent, handleDeviceOrientationChange, false);
		}
```

2. Request permission on iOS:
```503:518:demo.html
		function initDeviceOrientation() {
			if (DeviceOrientationEvent.requestPermission) {
				DeviceOrientationEvent.requestPermission()
					.then(response => {
						if (response == "granted") {
							tiltFBDelta = null;
							untrackDeviceOrientation(); // just in case it is subsequent attempt
							trackDeviceOrientation();
						}
					})
					.catch(console.error);
			} else {
				trackDeviceOrientation();
				tiltFBDelta = null;
			}
		}
```

3. Use the values in `onBeforeFrame`:
```588:594:demo.html
				if (tiltFBDelta) { // we have device orientation readings, better than mouse
					feedbackShiftVectorX = tiltLR * feedbackTiltShiftFactor;
					feedbackShiftVectorY += tiltFB * -feedbackTiltShiftFactor;
				} else {
					feedbackShiftVectorX = stMouseX * feedbackMouseShiftFactor;
					feedbackShiftVectorY += stMouseY * feedbackMouseShiftFactor;
				}
```

## Summary

- Mouse: use `mousemove` → convert with `ctx.toShaderX/Y()` → pass as uniforms
- Gyro: use `deviceorientation` → process `beta`/`gamma` → pass as uniforms
- The library provides `ctx.toShaderX()` and `ctx.toShaderY()` for coordinate conversion
- All interaction handling happens in your application code, typically in `onBeforeFrame` callbacks